  "observations": {
    "identityAndQuietRest": "Same substantial Crow bill/head silhouette, lowered legs, layered foldedwing masses and long tail read as the same quiet ground-rest individual. No stride/lifted foot, body repositioning or new pose is introduced. This is not a perched grip or animated rest.",
    "featherDetail": "Major overlappingwing feather edges and darker neck/mantle structure remain readable at186CSS. Relative to64colors, fine grayscale steps merge and largerflat tone clusters appear at cheek/neck and wing highlights; enlarged view is visibly coarser. Highresolution microfeather texture and fine contour shading are not fully retained. At normal size I do not see this loss turn the bird into an unreadable flat silhouette or a distracting posterized patch.",
    "eyeAndBill": "The small dark eye remains locatable under the brow in the head/bill context. Fine warm irisrim/catchlight from highresolution is weak or unresolved at186; twelvecolors further compresses subtle eye/cheek shading versus64. Do not describe individualiris detail as retained or infer a newclosed eye. Bill outline and darkmouth separation remain readable.",
    "feet": "Both footgroups remain visible and groundward; exact same alpha mask preserves their footprint and8connected silhouette, including the prior thresholdedclaw outline. Lower RGBshade resolution makes knuckle/toe separations and highlights less precise, especially darkondark. No additional toe pixel is geometrically removed in this palette step. Exact anatomicaltoe/sole/loadedsupport remains unproved.",
    "edgesAndContrast": "No new geometric halo, spur or detached part from the palette delta; silhouette alpha is bitidentical. Grayscale banding is visible enlarged but normal186light/dark rest appearance remains coherent. Prior79footfringe threshold loss remains inherited, not repaired.",
    "overallQualification": "Visually suitable as a provisional lowpalette static ground-rest source candidate, with explicit shading/eye/footdetail loss. Codecfit is not the visual acceptance reason."
  },
